From: Chokri Hammedi <52697989+blue0x1@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] graphics: GPU-shared guest memory (KYTY_GPU_SHARED_MEMORY=1)

The PS4's CPU and GPU share one RAM, like the PS5's. With
KYTY_GPU_SHARED_MEMORY=1 guest memory that game buffers use moves, in 32 MB
chunks, into dma-buf GPU memory (cached, host-coherent), mapped at the guest
addresses. Buffers there are aliased sparse Vulkan buffers over that memory:
no uploads, no page write tracking (mprotect) and no readbacks of
GPU-written data.

To keep the guest's view right:
- completion labels (RELEASE_MEM) reach guest memory when the GPU has
  finished, not when the command is read; the command processor, which runs
  ahead in order, still sees them at once (WAIT_REG_MEM);
- before the command processor reads a command buffer a shader wrote, it
  waits for that GPU work (games write commands from shaders);
- while a chunk moves its guest views are read-only; writers that fault wait.
- the GPU thread never waits for the guest memory lock to move a chunk: a
  map can hold it while waiting for the GPU thread (Invincible VS hung at
  start). The buffer stays a normal one and a later buffer moves the chunk.

Needs sparse aliased residency (RADV_EXPERIMENTAL=sparse) and dma-buf export.
Off by default. Teardown (PPSA15246) on a PS4 Pro: menu scene 2.1 -> 7 FPS,
in game 9-12 FPS; GPU waits per frame 72 -> 9.

Also, seen once Teardown ran long enough:
- the texture cache grows an image requested again at the same address with
  the same mips and layers but a larger size, instead of exiting;
- an image download larger than the download ring uses a one-off buffer.
